{"authored" [{"question" "q-278254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/65a76a438b3459a92db0e00b0c9bd281b8a9c58fa68afd2d8e6468a1d99b1cf9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0e36ba8271e2e827a6c40d37f976e956cdd830dd7da7aeb4e083adaf36cf30e" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278254" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278254" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{(x+1)^2}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^2}{16}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+5)^{2}}{11}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=-7$ and $k=-5,$ so the center is located at $(-7,-5).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=3$ and ${b}^{2}=11.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=3,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=11,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{11}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=\\sqrt{11},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{11}=2\\sqrt{11},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{3}=2\\sqrt{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f0e36ba8271e2e827a6c40d37f976e956cdd830dd7da7aeb4e083adaf36cf30e" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{11}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-278402" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b93e18213fdd67fce8b8bd708b1fe0842a7aca3225235ce4308e7e1682a8a1a" "basis" 630 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-7627" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981529/assets/2ab9745cb9d157e566c101a6ca79514017903fa57eaf8725c846afd272c74f93.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981529/state.json" "file_sha256" "7fa7bb211817ee62f856a7dab7c884db5d65bb38d660a4e8119b8e896b812d87" "basis" 892 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/1