on" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-115310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,-4)"] ["math" "(0,2)"] ["math" "(0,4)"] ["math" "(3,5)"] ["math" "(4,0)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-115310" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,-4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the ellipse $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{15}+\\frac{(y-6)^{2}}{49}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=1$ and $k=6,$ so the center is located at $(1,6).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have ${a}^{2}=15$ and ${b}^{2}=49.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=15,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=49,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{49}=7.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=7,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=\\sqrt{15}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 7=14,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot \\sqrt{15}=2\\sqrt{15}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "978ad4aecbb5087179a1a9a0986edf59e95f7dc95ee797b6f0b5822accb9c3f1"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "14"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "q-278403" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{15}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/state.json" "file_sha256" "583d196f49dcc52efd388e346e3d5f96473286b07fef0c6c4141991ce4beb062"} {"question" "e-5219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/assets/57ec3c089f80e2d35d9b83f6791eefca9b452d933f82209ed434d35d77b78788.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/example-5219.json" "file_sha256" "633cdc33e79960e5c2c06f3b2decd2f3a915f31913d05fa61e4795315c8c96e0"} {"question" "e-5219" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=5,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{25}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/example-5219.json" "file_sha256" "633cdc33e79960e5c2c06f3b2decd2f3a915f31913d05fa61e4795315c8c96e0"} {"question" "e-5222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/assets/b6eb2c0222181eaa4eb2a1c232fdf2eee905ab0e0a8a9c2284911ab8dd26cade.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/example-5222.json" "file_sha256" "3293b79736d790d73c73e443c715f5e4740acb5f07abdc080708eecab1201d02"} {"question" "e-5222" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3),$ the horizontal radius has length $a=2,$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-(-1))^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/example-5222.json" "file_sha256" "3293b79736d790d73c73e443c715f5e4740acb5f07abdc080708eecab1201d02"} {"question" "e-5223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1,$ find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/example-5223.json" "file_sha256" "3245f021146ed877730a4f64070e031a89ead4a39ed16cd9f870458549ea5b51"} {"question" "e-5223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2,$ so the center is located at $(2,-2).$\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16.$\n\n- Since $a^2=25,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\n- Since $b^2=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16} = 4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2\\cdot5 = 10,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2\\cdot4 = 8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038597/example-5223.json" "file_sha256" "3245f021146ed877730a4f64070e031a89ead4a39ed16cd9f870458549ea5b51"}] "usage" {"q-16461" [] "q-278254" [] "q-278402" [] "q-7627" [] "q-16555" [] "e-5223" [] "e-5219" [] "q-115310" [] "q-278401" [] "e-5222" []} "basis" 1316 "retractions" [[17592186087981 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/q-16555.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186267552 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13958359/assets/65a76a438b3459a92db0e00b0c9bd281b8a9c58fa68afd2d8e6468a1d99b1cf9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$"] [17592186088273 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981529/assets/2ab9745cb9d157e566c101a6ca79514017903fa57eaf8725c846afd272c74f93.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186087980 :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $((x - 1)^{2})/(8) + ((y + 2)^{2})/(4) = 1$?"] [17592186087464 :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/89500561af6affb81c697e1bffe36957.png)"] [17592186087464 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(1,-1)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$, and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(5^{2}) + ((y - (-1))^{2})/(3^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - 1)^{2})/(25) + ((y + 1)^{2})/(9) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087467 :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/849/Source/Images/0de79119b131777f87a4e2fdd6f3a262.png)"] [17592186087467 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the ellipse is centered at $(-1,-3)$, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$, and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((x - h)^{2})/(a^{2}) + ((y - k)^{2})/(b^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x - (-1))^{2})/(2^{2}) + ((y - (-3))^{2})/(4^{2}) &= 1 \\\\\n((x + 1)^{2})/(4) + ((y + 3)^{2})/(16) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087468 :question/problem "For the ellipse $\\dfrac{(x-2)^2}{25} + \\dfrac{(y+2)^2}{16} = 1$, find its center, and the lengths of its major and minor axes."] [17592186087468 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the general equation of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-h)^2}{{a}^2}+\\dfrac{(y-k)^2}{{b}^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $h=2$ and $k=-2$, so the center is located at $(2,-2)$.\n\nIn the given equation, we also have $a^2=25$ and $b^2=16$.\n\n- Since $a^2=25$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n- Since $b^